x = \frac{-(-11) \pm \sqrt{(-11)^2 - 4(-10)(6)}}{2(-10)} = \frac{11 \pm \sqrt{121 + 240}}{-20} = \frac{11 \pm \sqrt{361}}{-20} = \frac{11 \pm 19}{-20}
\] This gives us two solutions: \[
x = \frac{11 + 19}{-20} = \frac{30}{-20} = -\frac{3}{2} \quad \text{and} \quad x = \frac{11 - 19}{-20} = \frac{-8}{-20} = \frac{2}{5}
\] The roots of the quadratic equation are \(x = -\frac{3}{2}\) and \(x = \frac{2}{5}\). The quadratic expression \(-10x^2 - 11x + 6\) is a parabola opening downwards (since the coefficient of \(x^2\) is negative), so it is non-negative between the roots. Therefore, the domain of \(f(x)\) is the interval \(\left[-\frac{3}{2}, \frac{2}{5}\right]\). </think> <answer> \(\left[-\frac{3}{2}, \frac{2}{5}\right]\) </answer>

